'''thumb-latch''' (thum′lach), ''n.'' A kind of door-latch in which a lever passing through the door raises the latch. {{smaller|The lever is made to play from the outside by pressing upon the broadened end of it, generally with the thumb. See cut under ''latch''.}}

'''thumbless''' (thum′les), ''a.'' [< ''thumb''<sup>1</sup> + ''-less''.] 1. Having no thumbs: as, the thumbed and ''thumbless'' spider-monkeys. See ''Ateles'', ''Brachyteles'', and cut under ''spider-monkey''.—2. Having no hallux, or hind toe, as a bird.—3. Clumsy; awkward; unskilful.

{{smaller block|When to a house I come and see
The genius wastefull more than free;
The servants ''thumblesse'', yet to eat
With lawlesse tooth the floure of wheat.
{{right|''Herrick'', Leprosie in Houses.}}}}

'''thumb-mark''' (thum′märk), ''n.'' A mark left by the impression of the thumb, as on the leaves of a book; hence, any mark resembling this.

'''thumb-nut''' (thum′nut), ''n.'' A nut for a bolt or screw having wings which give a purchase to the thumb in turning it.

'''thumb-pad''' (thum′pad), ''n.'' A pad-like formation over the inner metacarpal bone of some batrachians.

